WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.139
then add the extract to water.

00:00:00.533 --> 00:00:02.626
A cloudy white emulsion is positive.

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:08.031
So starch is blue-black, reducing
sugar needs heat, protein is lilac,

00:00:08.221 --> 00:00:09.957
and lipid gives a white cloud.

00:00:10.358 --> 00:00:13.144
Four tests, not one magic color chart.

00:00:13.874 --> 00:00:14.713
Exactly.

00:00:15.105 --> 00:00:16.516
Keep the labels visible.

00:00:16.721 --> 00:00:20.797
Use suitable positive and negative
controls so you know the procedure

00:00:20.824 --> 00:00:22.179
and reagents are working.

00:00:22.712 --> 00:00:25.834
A water control should not give
a positive nutrient result.

00:00:26.605 --> 00:00:31.190
If two tubes have different colors,
can I announce exact grams of nutrient?

00:00:31.917 --> 00:00:33.890
Not from these simple observations alone.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.231
Some tests can give rough comparisons
under carefully controlled

00:00:37.231 --> 00:00:41.059
conditions, but exact amounts need
calibration and a suitable method.

00:00:41.437 --> 00:00:44.920
Color strength is not a universal
ruler for every food test.

00:00:45.788 --> 00:00:46.868
Same volumes,

00:00:46.997 --> 00:00:51.695
same conditions, clearly labeled
samples, and don't mix every

00:00:51.695 --> 00:00:53.303
chemical together to save time.

00:00:54.115 --> 00:00:55.132
Definitely not.

00:00:55.526 --> 00:00:58.030
These are supervised
school lab procedures.

00:00:58.328 --> 00:01:00.923
Wear eye protection and
follow instructions.

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:04.158
Ethanol is flammable and
stays away from flames.

00:01:04.371 --> 00:01:07.198
Hot water and reagents
need careful handling.

00:01:07.634 --> 00:01:09.700
Our animation shows results safely.

00:01:10.452 --> 00:01:12.904
I can remember the results
without building a chemical

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:14.163
cocktail in the kitchen.

00:01:14.604 --> 00:01:15.614
Back to our sandwich.

00:01:15.938 --> 00:01:20.910
If I tear the bread into tiny pieces, have
I changed starch molecules into sugar?

00:01:21.727 --> 00:01:25.431
Smaller pieces means
smaller molecules, so yes?

00:01:26.162 --> 00:01:26.720
Not yet.

00:01:27.104 --> 00:01:30.373
Mechanical digestion changes
the size of food pieces without

00:01:30.376 --> 00:01:31.980
chemically breaking the molecules.

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:35.137
Teeth cut and grind, the stomach churns.

00:01:35.688 --> 00:01:39.967
Chemical digestion uses enzymes to
break large molecules into smaller ones.

00:01:40.788 --> 00:01:42.382
So cutting a necklace

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:45.681
into shorter pieces of ribbon
is not the same as separating

00:01:45.681 --> 00:01:46.441
its beads?

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:48.565
Let's use a clearer model.

00:01:48.869 --> 00:01:51.735
These connected beads
represent a starch molecule.

00:01:52.137 --> 00:01:55.309
Crushing the bread separates
lumps containing many chains.

00:01:55.642 --> 00:01:57.928
Enzymes break bonds within the chains.

00:01:58.345 --> 00:02:01.219
The drawing is simplified,
but the distinction matters.

00:02:02.022 --> 00:02:05.288
Why bother chewing if enzymes
still have to do the chemistry?

00:02:06.083 --> 00:02:11.309
Smaller food pieces expose more surface
area, giving enzymes more places to act.

00:02:11.685 --> 00:02:14.316
That helps chemical
digestion proceed faster.

00:02:14.802 --> 00:02:18.616
It does not mean chewing has already
converted all the starch to glucose.

00:02:19.158 --> 00:02:20.881
Which teeth do which jobs?

00:02:21.703 --> 00:02:26.117
Incisors at the front cut and bite,
pointed canines grip and tear,

00:02:26.466 --> 00:02:30.176
premolars and molars have broader
surfaces for crushing and grinding.

00:02:30.517 --> 00:02:33.960
Saliva moistens the food and
contains amylase, beginning

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:35.531
starch digestion in the mouth.

00:02:36.335 --> 00:02:39.231
Then I swallow the softened ball of food.

00:02:39.666 --> 00:02:40.866
Does it fall to the stomach by

00:02:40.866 --> 00:02:41.454
gravity?

00:02:42.174 --> 00:02:44.912
Muscles in the esophagus
move it by peristalsis.

00:02:45.214 --> 00:02:48.136
Coordinated waves of
contraction push food along.

00:02:48.828 --> 00:02:52.215
Gravity can help when upright,
but it is not the only mechanism.

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:55.057
No upside down eating experiment, please.

00:02:55.756 --> 00:02:57.070
I was not volunteering.

00:02:57.872 --> 00:03:00.151
Can we trace the whole
route with the tube model?

00:03:00.906 --> 00:03:07.740
Mouth, esophagus, stomach, small
intestine, large intestine, rectum, anus.

00:03:08.121 --> 00:03:10.798
That continuous route
is the alimentary canal.

00:03:11.065 --> 00:03:14.758
Food does not take a sightseeing
detour through the liver or pancreas.

00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:20.512
The stomach churns and mixes, but
digestion does not start and finish there.

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:23.406
The mouth and small
intestine have jobs too

00:03:23.596 --> 00:03:24.953
What is an enzyme?

00:03:25.856 --> 00:03:28.690
A living worker that gets
used up after eating the food?

00:03:29.503 --> 00:03:31.477
Two imaginary teacher tears.

00:03:31.957 --> 00:03:34.343
An enzyme is a biological catalyst.

00:03:34.519 --> 00:03:38.343
It speeds up a chemical reaction
and is not used up by that reaction.

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:41.097
It is a molecule, not
a tiny living animal.

00:03:41.762 --> 00:03:46.819
One enzyme can work repeatedly, but
it cannot work on every food molecule?

00:03:47.624 --> 00:03:48.184
Correct.

00:03:48.547 --> 00:03:52.722
Enzymes are specific because their
active sites fit particular substrates.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:55.537
Amylase breaks starch into smaller sugars.

00:03:55.941 --> 00:03:58.608
Other enzymes finish
carbohydrate digestion to

00:03:58.609 --> 00:04:00.398
simple sugars such as glucose.

00:04:01.151 --> 00:04:05.626
So saying amylase alone turns
every starch molecule straight

00:04:05.626 --> 00:04:07.379
into glucose is too simple.

00:04:08.149 --> 00:04:08.847
Good catch.

00:04:09.213 --> 00:04:13.340
Proteases digest proteins,
ultimately producing amino acids.

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:15.450
Lipases digest fats.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:19.682
At this level, remember the products
as fatty acids and glycerol.

00:04:19.999 --> 00:04:22.399
The detailed pathway
has intermediate steps.

00:04:23.054 --> 00:04:25.182
Where do these enzymes come from?

00:04:26.028 --> 00:04:28.082
Salivary glands supply amylase.

00:04:28.427 --> 00:04:33.127
The stomach supplies a protease, and
the pancreas sends amylase, proteases,

00:04:33.147 --> 00:04:35.173
and lipase into the small intestine.

00:04:35.707 --> 00:04:39.096
Enzymes on the small intestine
lining help finish digestion.

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:40.000
And the stomach acid dissolves
everything like a cartoon vat?
